<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Ponthieu</h1><p class="st-meta">980 CE – 1369 CE (approx.) · capital Abbeville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small county at the mouth of the Somme where Harold Godwinson was held captive and where the English won at Crécy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ponthieu emerged around 980 when Hugh, a castellan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbeville">Abbeville</a>, married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> and was recognised as count. Abbeville became the seat of a modest dynasty on the coast between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Normandy</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts profited from the Somme estuary and the cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trades</a>. In 1064 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-count-of-ponthieu">Guy I</a> captured the shipwrecked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> and handed him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a>, as shown on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry</a>. In 1279 the county passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-castile">Eleanor of Castile</a> to her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The English kings held Ponthieu as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III</a> fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a>, inside the county, in 1346, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> of 1360 gave it to him in full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. French forces reconquered it in 1369, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>, later passing briefly to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-ponthieu.jpg" alt="County of Ponthieu" width="320" height="300"><figcaption>Image: ZigeunerAlt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cartecomtedeponthieu1180.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>980 CE – 1369 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Abbeville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guy I of Ponthieu, Joan of Dammartin, Edward I of England</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ponthieu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=county-of-ponthieu" aria-label="County of Ponthieu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4722 435 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Ponthieu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5042 691L5054 728M5042 691L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5042 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5042 691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Abbeville. <a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=county-of-ponthieu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

